---
import Presentation from "../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 14: Charting &amp; Trendlines</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 14</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Charting &amp; Trendlines</h3>
              <p><i>(Last session of the Foundations unit)</i></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why Plot?</h2>
              <p>
                A table of numbers can hide a lot &mdash; an outlier, a
                pattern, data that doesn't behave quite the way you expected.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">A plot makes it visible at a glance.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Building the Plot</h2>
              <ol>
                <li class="fragment">Scatter plot: weight vs. displacement</li>
                <li class="fragment">Add a linear trendline</li>
                <li class="fragment">Display the equation</li>
                <li class="fragment">Display R&sup2;</li>
              </ol>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Slope Tells You the Relationship</h2>
              <p>
                The slope of this line tells you the rate one thing changes
                relative to the other.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                For a launcher, that's the whole idea behind calibration.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>R&sup2; &mdash; What It Tells You</h2>
              <p>Closer to 1 = tighter fit.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>R&sup2; &mdash; What It Tells You</h2>
              <p>
                It's <b class="text-sn-theme">diagnostic information</b>, not
                a pass/fail grade &mdash; same idea as last session's
                conditional-formatting flags.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>If R&sup2; Is Low &mdash; Don't Just Move On</h2>
              <p>Ask:</p>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">Were a couple trials noisy? (measurement problem)</li>
                <li class="fragment">Does the data genuinely behave non-linearly here? (real effect)</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="fragment">Different problems, different fixes.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>You Could Also Compute This Directly</h2>
              <p><code>=SLOPE(y-range, x-range)</code></p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>You Could Also Compute This Directly</h2>
              <p><code>=RSQ(y-range, x-range)</code></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                The chart isn't magic &mdash; it's the same functions skill
                from Session 12, just drawn as a picture.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Submit Today</h2>
              <p>
                Data table + plot + trendline + R&sup2; + a one-sentence
                diagnostic note on your fit quality.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Watch Out For</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">Treating R&sup2; as a grade instead of information</li>
                <li class="fragment">Assuming low R&sup2; always means "redo the experiment"</li>
                <li class="fragment">Confusing the trendline's slope with individual data points</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>That's the Last New Skill in This Unit</h2>
              <p>
                Define, Design, Notebook, Safety, Measurement, Functions,
                Data Quality, Conversion, Tolerance, and now Charting &mdash;
                all of it was building toward one thing.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Up</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">The Launcher Project begins.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Same measure &rarr; log &rarr; chart workflow. Real stakes.
                Your own launcher.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Build the Plot</h3>
          <p>
            Using your Session 11&ndash;13 data, create a scatter plot of
            weight vs. displacement in Sheets.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Add the Trendline</h3>
          <p>
            Fit a linear trendline. Display its equation and its R&sup2;
            value.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Diagnose Your Fit</h3>
          <p>
            If your R&sup2; is low, don't just move on &mdash; decide whether
            it looks like a measurement problem (a couple of noisy trials) or
            a real effect (the data genuinely isn't linear across this
            range). If Session 13 flagged a row, check whether it's the
            source of any weakness in your fit.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Write Your Diagnostic Note</h3>
          <p>
            One sentence: is your fit strong or weak, and if weak, do you
            think that's from measurement noise or a real effect?
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Your submitted Sheets file must include all of the following:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Raw data table (from Sessions 11&ndash;13) &mdash; trials, weights, displacements, units</li>
            <li>Scatter plot of weight vs. displacement</li>
            <li>Trendline with displayed equation and R&sup2; value</li>
            <li>
              One-sentence diagnostic note: is your fit strong or weak, and
              if weak, do you think that's from measurement noise or a real
              effect?
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Is your fit strong or weak? Do you think that's from
              measurement noise or a real effect, and why?
            </li>
            <li>
              What would it take to convince you your fit was actually wrong,
              even if R&sup2; looked good?
            </li>
            <li>
              This is the last new skill in Foundations. Looking back at
              Define, Design, Notebook, Safety, Measurement, Functions, Data
              Quality, Conversion, Tolerance, and now Charting &mdash; which
              one do you think you'll rely on most once the Launcher Project
              starts?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your data table, plot, trendline, R&sup2;, and diagnostic note.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
